  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Today:\n\nOnshore flow mixing with low level moisture has manifested a large \nstratus deck that engulfs western Lake Superior this morning. This \ncloud deck continues to emanate from the Lake and has advected \ninland leading to a Dense Fog Advisory being needed for areas around \nthe Lake and inland portions of Carlton and St. Louis County. \nConditions are expected to improve as we head towards mid day and \nthe cloud deck retreats back to the Lake. Due to the presence of \nthis cloud deck we have modified the high temps a bit for the \nimmediate areas around Lake Superior. While most the region will \nlikely see temps in the 70s these areas may struggle to get out of \nthe 50 range. If the fog does retreat much faster than what we \ncurrently have in the forecast, expect this areas to climb into the \n60s. \n\nThe latter half of the day will see a warm front lifting northward \nthrough the day. Isentropic lift associated with the moving boundary \nwill be best concentrated across our our western counties this \nafternoon where we have PoPs of 40-60%. In conjunction with the warm \nfront there will be some added forcing as a shortwave rotates aloft \nclearing the way of the subsidence aloft. Minimal instability \navailable with the HREF showing a 15% chance of an occasional rumble \nof thunder. Rain totals will be light as this feature swings \nsouthwest to northeast during the overnight hours. \n\nSATURDAY:\n\nOn Saturday a mature low pressure system propagates eastward across \nthe northern Canadian Prairies and into far northern Ontario. It's \nassociated cold front will be cruising east across the Northland \nproviding another dose of light rain chances and perhaps a rumble of \nthunder across the region. MUCAPE increases a bit along the boundary \nas upper-levels cool and mid-level lapse rates increase to 7C/km. \nThis could lead to some isolated storms at times but not expecting \nanything severe. Rain chances will quickly taper off west to east \nthrough Saturday night as the boundary moves into central WI. \nAdditionally, out ahead of this boundary through the day the strong \nsoutherly flow will lead to widespread 70s, our last day of 15-25 \ndegrees above normal for a while.\n\nSUNDAY THRU NEXT THURSDAY:\n\nA return to more seasonable temperatures as surface high pressure \nsettles in behind the exiting cold front. Highs will be in the 40s \nand 50s with overnight lows dropping below freezing. A more active \npattern still looks to be in store for the Northland as we head into \nnext week. The cool and dry air mass will help to shunt much of the \nactivity to our south initially but an upper level low is forecasted \nto eject out of the Central Rockies Monday through Tuesday. The \nexact path and timing are still a bit in flux between model runs. \nBut some of the thermodynamics would suggest enough cold air \naloft to mix in some snow on the northern fringes of the system.\nThis presently does not look to amount to much aside from \n\"conversational snow.\"",
